EXAM_DATA.papers["2019-english-one"] = {"id":"2019-english-one","year":2019,"label":"2019 英语一","era":"modern","sourceUrl":"https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/kaoyan/paper/2019-english-one/","timeLimitMin":180,"scoreMode":"estimated","parts":[{"id":"section1","kind":"cloze","title":"Section I 完形填空","directions":"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section1-1","text":"Today we live in a world where GPS systems, digital maps, and other navigation apps are available on our smartphones.   {{1}}   of us just walk straight into the woods without a phone. But phones   {{2}}   on batteries, and batteries can die faster than we realize.   {{3}}   you get lost without a phone or a compass, and you   {{4}}   can't find north, we have a few tricks to help you navigate   {{5}}   to civilization, one of which is to follow the land."},{"id":"p-section1-2","text":"When you find yourself well   {{6}}   a trail, but not in a completely   {{7}}   area, you have to answer two questions: Which   {{8}}   is downhill, in this particular area? And where is the nearest water source? Humans overwhelmingly live in valleys, and on supplies of fresh water.   {{9}}   , if you head downhill, and follow any H2O you find, you should   {{10}}   see signs of people."},{"id":"p-section1-3","text":"If you've explored the area before, keep an eye out for familiar sights – you may be   {{11}}   how quickly identifying a distinctive rock or tree can restore your bearings."},{"id":"p-section1-4","text":"Another   {{12}}   : Climb high and look for signs of human habitation.   {{13}}   , even in dense forest, you should be able to   {{14}}   gaps in the tree line due to roads, train tracks, and other paths people carve   {{15}}   the woods. Head toward these   {{16}}   to find a way out. At night, scan the horizon for   {{17}}   light sources, such as fires and streetlights, then walk toward the glow of light pollution."},{"id":"p-section1-5","text":"{{18}}   , assuming you're lost in an area humans tend to frequent, look for the   {{19}}   we leave on the landscape. Trail blazes, tire tracks, and other features can   {{20}}   you to civilization."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Some"},{"key":"B","text":"Most"},{"key":"C","text":"Few"},{"key":"D","text":"All"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"1.","reason":"后文 But 转折反推此句应是少数 线索在紧跟的下一句 But phones __(2)__ on batteries, and batteries can die faster than we realize. 这个 But 提示前句在说“几乎人人都带着手机”，所以本空句 __(1)__ of us just walk straight into the woods without a phone. 应表达“很少有人不带手机就进林子”，答案要指向“少数”。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Some 表“一些”，与下文“手机普及、电量会耗尽”的强对比衔接不上，没有 Few 那种“极少有人不带手机”的反差力度。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Most 是“大多数人不带手机进林子”，与“今天人人都靠手机导航”的事实正好相反，逻辑指向错误。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Few 意为“很少有人”，正好承接“手机导航极其普及”的语境——很少有人不带手机就进林子，再由 But 引出隐患，逻辑链顺畅。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"All 表“所有人都不带手机进林子”，既绝对又与事实相反，是范围/方向双重错误。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":2,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"put"},{"key":"B","text":"take"},{"key":"C","text":"run"},{"key":"D","text":"come"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"2.","reason":"run on = 靠……运转 线索就在本句 phones __(2)__ on batteries。要表达“手机靠电池运转”，英语固定说法是 run on（靠某能源运转），指向能与 on batteries 构成“依靠……驱动”含义的动词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"put on 意为“穿上/上演/增加”，不能表示“靠电池供电”，搭配义不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"take on 意为“承担/呈现/雇用”，与“靠电池运转”无关，属搭配错误。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"run 与 on 构成 run on，正表示“（设备）靠某种能源运转”，run on batteries 即“靠电池供电”，搭配与语义双对。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"come on 意为“快点/出场/进展”，不能表达“依靠电池运转”，搭配不当。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":3,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Since"},{"key":"B","text":"If"},{"key":"C","text":"Though"},{"key":"D","text":"Until"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"3.","reason":"迷路是假设、给招是结果 整句为 __(3)__ you get lost without a phone or a compass, and you __(4)__ can't find north, we have a few tricks to help you navigate。从句“迷路、找不到北”是一种尚未发生的假设情形，主句“我们有几个窍门”是应对方案，二者是“假设条件 → 结果”，线索指向条件连词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Since 表“既然/因为”，会把“迷路”当成已发生的既定原因，但这里是面向读者的假设情景，并非陈述事实，因果不成立。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"If 引导条件状语从句，“如果你没手机没指南针而迷了路、找不到北”，正是给出技巧的前提情境，逻辑最贴。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Though 表让步“尽管”，需要主从句存在转折对立，但“迷路”与“有窍门相助”不是对立关系，是条件与应对，逻辑不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Until 表“直到……为止”，代入“直到你迷路，我们才有窍门”时间逻辑荒谬，不通。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":4,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"formally"},{"key":"B","text":"relatively"},{"key":"C","text":"gradually"},{"key":"D","text":"literally"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"4.","reason":"强调‘真的连北都找不到’ 本句 you __(4)__ can't find north 接在“没手机没指南针”之后，是在强调“连最基本的方向都确确实实找不到”这种实打实的窘境，线索指向表“真正地/确实地”的副词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"formally 意为“正式地/形式上”，与“找不到北”这一实际困境无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"relatively 表“相对地”，会变成“相对找不到北”，反而削弱了此处要强调的绝对窘迫感，不符。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"gradually 表“逐渐地”，描述的是缓慢过程，而“找不到北”是一个状态判断，时间义不搭。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"literally 表“字面上/确实地”，强化“你真的、连方向都辨不出”的处境，与前面“没手机没指南针”的绝境呼应，语气最贴。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":5,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"back"},{"key":"B","text":"next"},{"key":"C","text":"around"},{"key":"D","text":"away"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"5.","reason":"迷路后是‘返回’文明 线索在全句语境：人已迷失在林中，目标是 to help you navigate __(5)__ to civilization，即“回到文明社会”。从有人烟处走丢、再设法走回去，方向是“返回”，指向 back to。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"back 与 to 构成 back to，“回到文明社会”，正合“迷路后想方设法走回去”的语境，方向与搭配双对。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"next 与 to 构成 next to“紧挨着”，表位置而非“朝文明前进”，与“navigate（导航前往）”搭不上。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"around 表“四处/绕着”，没有“返回目的地”之意，与 to civilization 的“奔向某处”逻辑不合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"away 表“离开”，away to 是远离方向，与“回到文明”正好相反，方向错误。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":6,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"onto"},{"key":"B","text":"off"},{"key":"C","text":"across"},{"key":"D","text":"alone"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"6.","reason":"与‘但不在完全陌生处’呼应 线索在同句对比 When you find yourself well __(6)__ a trail, but not in a completely __(7)__ area。后半 but 说“但还没到完全陌生的地方”，前半应是“已明显偏离了小径”，off a trail 即“偏离/脱离小径”，方向指向 off。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"onto 表“到……之上”，well onto a trail 意为“稳稳走在小径上”，那就不存在迷路，与全段“迷路自救”矛盾。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"off 与 a trail 构成 off a trail“偏离/脱离了小径”，well off 强调“偏得挺远”，正合“已离开正道但又没到完全陌生处”的处境。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"across 表“横穿”，across a trail 是“横穿小径”，描述瞬时动作而非“迷失偏离”的状态，不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"alone 意为“独自”，与方位无关，且 well alone a trail 不成结构，属形近/词性干扰（易与 along 混淆）。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":7,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"unattractive"},{"key":"B","text":"uncrowded"},{"key":"C","text":"unchanged"},{"key":"D","text":"unfamiliar"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"7.","reason":"承接 off a trail 的‘陌生’ 线索在前半句 When you find yourself well __(6)__ a trail, but not in a completely __(7)__ area。空 6 已定“偏离了小径”而迷路，作者要说的是“但又不至于完全陌生（还有线索可循）”，指向 unfamiliar。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"unattractive 表“不吸引人的”，谈的是景色美丑，与“迷路辨向”话题无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"uncrowded 表“不拥挤的”，讲人多人少，而本句关心的是“认不认得这片地方”，维度错位。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"unchanged 表“未变化的”，需要一个“之前的状态”作参照，此处没有这种前后对比，逻辑无着落。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"unfamiliar 表“陌生的”，“偏离了小径但又不是完全陌生的区域”正说明还有熟悉线索可用，与下文“留意熟悉景物”遥相呼应，语义最贴。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":8,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"site"},{"key":"B","text":"point"},{"key":"C","text":"way"},{"key":"D","text":"place"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"8.","reason":"问的是‘哪个方向是下坡’ 线索在本句的两个并列问题 Which __(8)__ is downhill, in this particular area? And where is the nearest water source?。第一问要确定“哪条路/哪个方向通向下坡”，way 表“方向/路线”，which way 是地道说法，指向 way。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"site 指“场所/遗址”，which site 问的是哪个地点，而本句要问的是“哪个方向往下坡”，答非所问。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"point 表“点/地点/要点”，which point 不表“朝哪个方向”，与 is downhill 搭配生硬。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"way 表“方向/路线”，which way is downhill 即“哪个方向是下坡”，正是要判断的逃生方向，搭配地道、语义精准。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"place 表“地方”，which place 问哪个地点，无法表达“朝哪个方向下坡”，方向义缺失。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":9,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"So"},{"key":"B","text":"Yet"},{"key":"C","text":"Instead"},{"key":"D","text":"Besides"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"9.","reason":"前句给依据、本句出结论 线索在前一句 Humans overwhelmingly live in valleys, and on supplies of fresh water. 它给出事实依据；本句 __(9)__, if you head downhill, and follow any H2O you find, you should __(10)__ see signs of people. 据此推出做法。前因后果，指向表“因此/所以”的 So。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"So 表“因此/所以”，承接“人类多住在山谷和淡水边”这一前提，推出“朝下坡走、顺水找，就该看到人迹”，因果链完整。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Yet 表转折“然而”，但前后句是顺承因果、并无对立，转折无从谈起。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Instead 表“相反/作为替代”，需要前文有一个被否定/被替换的做法，此处没有，逻辑不成立。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Besides 表“此外”，引出并列新增信息，但本句是对前句的推论而非另起一点，递进关系不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":10,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"immediately"},{"key":"B","text":"intentionally"},{"key":"C","text":"unexpectedly"},{"key":"D","text":"eventually"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"10.","reason":"‘走、跟着水’是渐进过程 线索在本句的条件 if you head downhill, and follow any H2O you find, you should __(10)__ see signs of people。下坡、沿水而行是一个需要时间的过程，结果不会“立刻”出现，而是“最终/终究”会看到人迹，指向 eventually。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"immediately 表“立刻”，但下坡沿水而行需要时间，不可能马上看到人迹，时间逻辑不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"intentionally 表“有意地”，“有意看到人迹”说不通——看到人迹是结果而非可刻意为之的动作。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"unexpectedly 表“出乎意料地”，但句中 should 表示“按规律应当会”，是可预期的结果，与‘意外’矛盾。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"eventually 表“最终/终究”，照此走下去“终会看到人迹”，与“下坡沿水”这一渐进过程及 should 的可预期语气都吻合。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"surprised"},{"key":"B","text":"annoyed"},{"key":"C","text":"frightened"},{"key":"D","text":"confused"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"11.","reason":"‘多快就能恢复方向感’是积极意外 线索在 you 后的从句 you may be __(11)__ how quickly identifying a distinctive rock or tree can restore your bearings。“认出一块特别的石头或树竟能这么快帮你恢复方向感”是一个出人意料的好结果，情绪应是“惊讶/惊喜”，指向 surprised。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"surprised 表“惊讶的”，“你也许会惊讶于认出一块奇石或一棵树竟能这么快恢复方向感”，与 how quickly 的‘出乎意料’语气完美匹配。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"annoyed 表“恼怒的”，是负面情绪，但“这么快恢复方向感”是好事，色彩相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"frightened 表“害怕的”，恢复方向感本是缓解恐惧之事，不会令人害怕，逻辑反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"confused 表“困惑的”，而本句正是说“方向感被恢复”，是从困惑走向清晰，情绪自相矛盾。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"problem"},{"key":"B","text":"option"},{"key":"C","text":"view"},{"key":"D","text":"result"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"12.","reason":"前文是一条条‘自救办法’ 线索在前几段的内容性质：第2段“跟着地形走”、第3段“认熟悉景物”都是自救办法，本段 Another __(12)__: Climb high and look for signs of human habitation. 冒号后又给出一条做法“登高找人类居住迹象”，说明 Another 概括的是“又一个办法/选择”，指向 option。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"problem 表“问题”，但冒号后是建设性的做法“登高找人烟”，是在给办法而非提问题，定性相反。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"option 表“选择/可选办法”，与前文逐条给出的自救招数同类，“又一个办法：登高找人类居住迹象”，归类精准。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"view 表“观点/景色”，冒号后既非一种看法也非风景描写，而是一项行动建议，词义不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"result 表“结果”，但“登高找人烟”是要采取的手段而非已产生的结果，逻辑颠倒。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Above all"},{"key":"B","text":"In contrast"},{"key":"C","text":"On average"},{"key":"D","text":"For example"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"13.","reason":"本句是对‘登高找人迹’的具体例示 线索在上句的概括 Climb high and look for signs of human habitation. 与本句的具体化 __(13)__, even in dense forest, you should be able to __(14)__ gaps in the tree line due to roads, train tracks, and other paths。本句用“树线缺口、公路、铁轨”等具体例子来落实上句的笼统建议，是举例关系，指向 For example。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Above all 表“最重要的是”，需要在多点中突出最关键一条，但本句只是对上句的具体展开，并无主次排序，不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"In contrast 表“相比之下/相反”，本句与上句是一致的延伸而非对立，转折关系不成立。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"On average 表“平均而言”，用于统计/数据语境，此处谈的是具体辨认方法，没有平均量可言，不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"For example 表“例如”，用“密林中也能看到因公路、铁轨形成的树线缺口”这一具体情形来例示“登高找人类迹象”，概括—举例关系成立。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"bridge"},{"key":"B","text":"avoid"},{"key":"C","text":"spot"},{"key":"D","text":"separate"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"14.","reason":"目的是‘发现’缺口以找出路 线索在前句目的 Climb high and look for signs of human habitation. 与本句 you should be able to __(14)__ gaps in the tree line，以及后句 Head toward these __(16)__ to find a way out.。登高是为了“看到/发现”这些缺口再朝它走，动作应是“发现”，指向 spot。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"bridge 作动词表“架桥/弥合”，对“树线缺口”架桥说不通，与‘找出路’目的无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"avoid 表“避开”，但后句明说要 Head toward these（朝缺口走去），避开正好相反，逻辑冲突。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"spot 表“发现/认出”，承接前句 look for，“你应能发现树线中的缺口”，再朝它走去找出路，动作目的链完整。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"separate 表“分开/隔开”，把缺口分开没有意义，与情境不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"from"},{"key":"B","text":"through"},{"key":"C","text":"beyond"},{"key":"D","text":"under"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"15.","reason":"小路是‘穿过’树林开出的 线索在本句 roads, train tracks, and other paths people carve __(15)__ the woods。公路、铁轨、小道都是“穿越树林”而开辟出来的，path through the woods 是地道说法，指向 through。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"from 表“从……（出发）”，carve from 多指“从某材料中雕出”，与“小路穿过树林”的空间关系不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"through 表“穿过”，paths people carve through the woods 即“人们穿过树林开辟出的小路”，空间关系与搭配都地道。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"beyond 表“超出/在……那边”，小路是‘贯穿’树林而非在树林之外，方位不合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"under 表“在……下面”，小路并非开在树林下方，空间关系错误。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"posts"},{"key":"B","text":"links"},{"key":"C","text":"shades"},{"key":"D","text":"breaks"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"16.","reason":"these 复指上句的 gaps 线索在上句 you should be able to __(14)__ gaps in the tree line 与本句 Head toward these __(16)__ to find a way out。these ___ 复指那些“树线缺口”，需要一个与 gaps 同义的词——breaks 即“断口/缺口”，指向 breaks。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"posts 表“柱子/帖子/岗位”，与上句的 gaps（缺口）不同义，无法复指。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"links 表“连接/纽带”，强调‘相连’，而 gaps 强调‘断开/空缺’，语义恰相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"shades 表“阴影/深浅/遮阳物”，与“树线缺口”无关，不能复指。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"breaks 作名词表“断裂/缺口”，与上句 gaps 同义，these breaks 即“这些缺口”，朝它们走去找出路，复指准确。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"artificial"},{"key":"B","text":"mysterious"},{"key":"C","text":"hidden"},{"key":"D","text":"limited"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"17.","reason":"such as fires and streetlights 是人造光 线索在 such as 的举例 scan the horizon for __(17)__ light sources, such as fires and streetlights, then walk toward the glow of light pollution。火光、路灯都是人造光源，且末尾点明 light pollution（光污染，人类活动所致），统领词应是“人造的”，指向 artificial。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"artificial 表“人造的”，火光、路灯正是人造光源，且与 light pollution 呼应——找人造光意味着附近有人，语义精准。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"mysterious 表“神秘的”，但目的是借光找到人烟，光源越‘指示有人’越好，神秘与否不相干。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"hidden 表“隐藏的”，但要 scan the horizon（扫视地平线）去找的应是看得见的光，‘隐藏的光’自相矛盾。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"limited 表“有限的”，描述数量/范围，与‘光是否由人造、能否指示人烟’这一关键属性无关。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Finally"},{"key":"B","text":"Consequently"},{"key":"C","text":"Incidentally"},{"key":"D","text":"Generally"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"18.","reason":"全文并列多招、此为最后一招 线索在全文结构：前面已分条给出‘跟地形’‘认景物’‘登高找光’等办法，本段 __(18)__, assuming you're lost in an area humans tend to frequent, look for the __(19)__ we leave on the landscape. 是最后一条建议并收尾，指向表‘最后/最终’的 Finally。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Finally 表“最后”，承接前面一系列并列办法，引出最后一招‘找人留在地貌上的痕迹’并收尾，符合全文‘罗列若干技巧’的结构。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Consequently 表“因此”，需要本段是前文的结果，但本段是又一条并列建议，不是推论，因果不成立。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Incidentally 表“顺便说一句”，引出离题的次要信息，但本段是与前文同等重要的一条正经办法，语气不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Generally 表“一般而言”，用于概括普遍情况，但此处是补上最后一条具体办法并收束全文，并非泛化总结。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"memories"},{"key":"B","text":"marks"},{"key":"C","text":"notes"},{"key":"D","text":"belongings"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"19.","reason":"下句举例 Trail blazes, tire tracks 都是‘痕迹’ 线索在紧跟的下一句 Trail blazes, tire tracks, and other features can __(20)__ you to civilization.。路标、轮胎印这些都是人类留在地面上的‘痕迹/印记’，反推空处的中心词应是‘痕迹’，指向 marks。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"memories 表“记忆”，是抽象心理之物，无法 leave on the landscape（留在地貌上），搭配不通。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"marks 表“痕迹/印记”，正好统领下句的路标、轮胎印等具体例子，“找我们留在地貌上的痕迹”，搭配与语义双对。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"notes 表“笔记/便条”，下句的胎印、路标并非‘便条’，无法被它涵盖，范围不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"belongings 表“随身财物”，路标、胎印不是‘遗落的物品’，而是留下的印记，词义不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"restrict"},{"key":"B","text":"adopt"},{"key":"C","text":"lead"},{"key":"D","text":"expose"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"20.","reason":"与首段‘navigate back to civilization’呼应 线索既在本句 Trail blazes, tire tracks, and other features can __(20)__ you to civilization.，也回扣首段目标 help you navigate __(5)__ to civilization。这些痕迹能“引领你走向文明社会”，lead sb to 是地道搭配，指向 lead。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"restrict 表“限制”，restrict sb to 是‘把某人限制在’，与‘引领走向文明’的积极方向相反。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"adopt 表“采纳/收养”，不接 ‘sb to 地点’的框架，‘adopt you to civilization’不成结构。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"lead 与 to 构成 lead sb to，“把你引向文明社会”，正合“循着痕迹找到人烟”的结论，呼应首段目标，搭配与语义双对。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"expose 表“使暴露”，搭配是 expose sb to sth（使接触某物），‘使你暴露于文明’语义别扭，不合此处‘带你回到文明’之意。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-1","kind":"reading","title":"Text 1","textNo":1,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 1","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-1","text":"Financial regulators in Britain have imposed a rather unusual rule on the bosses of big banks. Starting next year, any guaranteed bonus of top executives could be delayed 10 years if their banks are under investigation for wrongdoing. The main purpose of this \"clawback\" rule is to hold bankers accountable for harmful risk-taking and to restore public trust in financial institutions. Yet officials also hope for a much larger benefit: more long-term decision-making, not only by banks but by all corporations, to build a stronger economy for future generations."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-2","text":"\"Short-termism\" or the desire for quick profits, has worsened in publicly traded companies, says the Bank of England's top economist, Andrew Haldane. He quotes a giant of classical economics, Alfred Marshall, in describing this financial impatience as acting like \"children who pick the plums out of their pudding to eat them at once\" rather than putting them aside to be eaten last."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-3","text":"The average time for holding a stock in both the United States and Britain, he notes, has dropped from seven years to seven months in recent decades. Transient investors, who demand high quarterly profits from companies, can hinder a firm's efforts to invest in long-term research or to build up customer loyalty. This has been dubbed \"quarterly capitalism.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-4","text":"In addition, new digital technologies have allowed more rapid trading of equities, quicker use of information, and thus shorter attention spans in financial markets. \"There seems to be a predominance of short-term thinking at the expense of long-term investing,\" said Commissioner Daniel Gallagher of the US Securities and Exchange Commission in a speech this week."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-5","text":"In the US, the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 has pushed most public companies to defer performance bonuses for senior executives by about a year, slightly helping reduce \"short-termism.\" In its latest survey of CEO pay, The Wall Street Journal finds that \"a substantial part\" of executive pay is now tied to performance."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-6","text":"Much more could be done to encourage \"long-termism,\" such as changes in the tax code and quicker disclosure of stock acquisitions. In France, shareholders who hold onto a company investment for at least two years can sometimes earn more voting rights in a company."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-7","text":"Within companies, the right compensation design can provide incentives for executives to think beyond their own time at the company and on behalf of all stakeholders. Britain's new rule is a reminder to bankers that society has an interest in their performance, not just for the short term but for the long term."}]},"questions":[{"n":21,"stem":"According to Paragraph 1, one motive in imposing the new rule is to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"enhance bankers' sense of responsibility."},{"key":"B","text":"help corporations achieve larger profits."},{"key":"C","text":"build a new system of financial regulation."},{"key":"D","text":"guarantee the bonuses of top executives."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第一段,实施新规则的一个动机是____","choicesZh":{"A":"增强银行家的责任感。","B":"帮助企业实现更大的利润。","C":"建立新的金融监管体系。","D":"保证高管的奖金。"},"analysis":{"type":"According to Paragraph 1, one motive in imposing the new rule is to____ 把范围直接锁在一段之内，问一个具体动机——典型的段内细节题。","parse":"句子主干是 one motive ... is to____，主系表结构，待补全部分是 to 不定式，做表语。介词短语 in imposing the new rule 修饰 motive，说明动机针对的是哪件事。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"The main purpose of this \"clawback\" rule is to hold bankers accountable for harmful risk-taking and to restore public trust in financial institutions.","locateZh":"这条「追回」规则的主要目的，是让银行家为有害的冒险行为承担责任，并恢复公众对金融机构的信任。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判「让银行家担责」与 enhance bankers' sense of responsibility 是同一语义的两种说法，无须二次推理即可锁定。","pairs":[{"orig":"hold bankers accountable for harmful risk-taking","opt":"enhance bankers' sense of responsibility"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文远期愿景是 to build a stronger economy for future generations——是「更强的经济」，而非帮企业「赚更多利润」，把宏观经济目标偷换成微观利润目标。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文说监管者 have imposed a rather unusual rule——只是在既有体系上加一条规则，并未「构建一套新的金融监管体系」。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文是 any guaranteed bonus of top executives could be delayed 10 years——奖金被「延迟」发放，是约束高管的手段，目的恰恰是让他们忌惮，而不是去「保障」他们的奖金。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":22,"stem":"Alfred Marshall is quoted to indicate____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the conditions for generating quick profits."},{"key":"B","text":"governments' impatience in decision-making."},{"key":"C","text":"the solid structure of publicly traded companies."},{"key":"D","text":"\"short-termism\" in economic activities."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"引用Alfred Marshall是为了说明____","choicesZh":{"A":"产生快速利润的条件。","B":"政府在决策中的急躁。","C":"上市公司的稳固结构。","D":"经济活动中的\"短期主义\"。"},"analysis":{"type":"Alfred Marshall is quoted to indicate____ 直问「引用为了表明什么」——典型的例证/引证目的题。","parse":"被动句：Alfred Marshall is quoted to indicate____，主语是被引者本人，is quoted 是被动谓语，to indicate 是不定式做目的状语，问的是「谁引他、为了表明什么」。结构无陷阱。","locateEn":"\"Short-termism\" or the desire for quick profits, has worsened in publicly traded companies, says the Bank of England's top economist, Andrew Haldane. He quotes a giant of classical economics, Alfred Marshall, in describing this financial impatience as acting like \"children who pick the plums out of their pudding to eat them at once\" rather than putting them aside to be eaten last.","locateZh":"英格兰银行首席经济学家 Andrew Haldane 说，「短期主义」——也就是追逐快钱的欲望——在上市公司中已日益严重。他援引古典经济学巨匠 Alfred Marshall 的话，把这种「金融上的不耐烦」描述为「孩子们把布丁里的李子先挑出来吃掉」，而不愿把它们留到最后吃。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文是用比喻批评追逐 quick profits 的心态，根本没在谈「产生快钱需要什么条件」，选项把负面被批的对象偷换成正面的方法论。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文聚焦 publicly traded companies 的短期主义，从未提及 governments；把主语从公司张冠到政府，无中生有。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文是 has worsened in publicly traded companies——上市公司里短期主义正在恶化，根本不是说其结构 solid。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Haldane 借 Marshall 的 children 比喻，是要让读者直观感受 short-termism 的本质（贪图眼前、不愿等待），所以答案必然回到 short-termism 自身——精准对应 D。","pairs":[{"orig":"\"Short-termism\" or the desire for quick profits, has worsened in publicly traded companies","opt":"\"short-termism\" in economic activities"}]}}}}},{"n":23,"stem":"It is argued that the influence of transient investment on public companies can be____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"indirect."},{"key":"B","text":"adverse."},{"key":"C","text":"minimal."},{"key":"D","text":"temporary."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"推断题","stemZh":"有人认为短期投资对上市公司的影响可能是____","choicesZh":{"A":"间接的。","B":"不利的。","C":"极小的。","D":"暂时的。"},"analysis":{"type":"It is argued that the influence of transient investment on public companies can be____ 的 argued 加 can be，把客观陈述转成「作者立场—判断」问法，需对定位句的情感极性做一步推断。","parse":"形式主语句：It is argued that... 中 It 是形式主语，that 从句是真主语；从句主干 the influence ... can be____，两个介词短语 of transient investment 和 on public companies 分别说明「谁的影响」和「作用于谁」，待填位置是表语形容词。","locateEn":"Transient investors, who demand high quarterly profits from companies, can hinder a firm's efforts to invest in long-term research or to build up customer loyalty.","locateZh":"短期投资者要求公司创造高额季度利润，他们会阻碍企业投入长期研发或培养客户忠诚度的努力。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Transient investors ... can hinder a firm's efforts 中 hinder 的施动者就是 Transient investors，宾语是 firm's efforts，主谓宾一线相连，不存在中间环节，谈不上「间接」。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"adverse 即「有害的、不利的」，与 hinder + long-term research / customer loyalty 的语义方向完全一致，是预判的形容词版本。","pairs":[{"orig":"can hinder a firm's efforts to invest in long-term research or to build up customer loyalty","opt":"adverse"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文用 can hinder ... long-term research ... customer loyalty——两件企业的核心战略事项被妨碍，怎么可能是 minimal；与原文强度相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"The average time for holding a stock in both the United States and Britain ... has dropped from seven years to seven months in recent decades——是几十年的结构性变化，影响绝非「暂时」。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":24,"stem":"The US and France examples are used to illustrate____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the obstacles to preventing \"short-termism\"."},{"key":"B","text":"the significance of long-term thinking."},{"key":"C","text":"the approaches to promoting \"long-termism\"."},{"key":"D","text":"the prevalence of short-term thinking."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"美国和法国的例子用来说明____","choicesZh":{"A":"防止\"短期主义\"的障碍。","B":"长期思维的重要性。","C":"促进\"长期主义\"的方法。","D":"短期思维的盛行。"},"analysis":{"type":"The US and France examples are used to illustrate____ 直问举例的目的，与 22 题同型。","parse":"被动句：The US and France examples are used to illustrate____，主语是两个并列专名做定语的 examples，are used 是被动谓语，to illustrate 是不定式做目的状语；待填部分是 illustrate 的宾语，即「举例所服务的论点」。","locateEn":"Much more could be done to encourage \"long-termism,\" such as changes in the tax code and quicker disclosure of stock acquisitions. In France, shareholders who hold onto a company investment for at least two years can sometimes earn more voting rights in a company.","locateZh":"在鼓励「长期主义」上，可做的事还有很多，比如调整税法、加快股票交易披露。在法国，持有公司股份至少两年的股东有时可以获得更多投票权。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文两个例子全部展示「已经做了/可以做」的正向措施，没提任何 obstacle；选项把「促进路径」偷换为「阻碍因素」。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文是给出 tax code、quicker disclosure、voting rights 等具体做法——回答「怎么做」；选项却答「长期思考的重要性」——回答「为什么重要」，两个问号不同。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判「促进长期主义的办法」，与 the approaches to promoting \"long-termism\" 的 approaches 完全对应；美法两例正是这种 approaches 的实例。","pairs":[{"orig":"Much more could be done to encourage \"long-termism\"","opt":"the approaches to promoting \"long-termism\""}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"两个例子讲的是美法各自鼓励长期主义的措施，根本不是在讨论「短期思考多普遍」——主题方向完全反了。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":25,"stem":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Failure of Quarterly Capitalism"},{"key":"B","text":"Patience as a Corporate Virtue"},{"key":"C","text":"Decisiveness Required of Top Executives"},{"key":"D","text":"Frustration of Risk-taking Bankers"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"以下哪项是本文的最佳标题?","choicesZh":{"A":"季度资本主义的失败","B":"耐心作为企业美德","C":"高管所需的果断性","D":"冒险银行家的挫折"},"analysis":{"type":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text? 是主旨题的最典型问法。","parse":"特殊疑问句：Which of the following would be the best title for the text?，主语 Which，谓语 would be，表语 the best title，of the following 是 Which 的后置定语限定选项范围。结构无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文只说 quarterly capitalism 有害（This has been dubbed \"quarterly capitalism.\" 紧接 hinder 一句），并未宣布它 fail；且只占 P3 一段的局部，无法覆盖 P5-P6 的「长期主义办法」分支。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Patience 对应 long-term，Corporate 对应文中反复出现的 companies/corporations，Virtue 对应作者把 long-termism 定为好品质的立场——三个词分别承担覆盖、范围、极性三个维度，是四个选项中唯一全部命中的。","pairs":[{"orig":"Yet officials also hope for a much larger benefit: more long-term decision-making","opt":"Patience as a Corporate Virtue"},{"orig":"not just for the short term but for the long term","opt":"Patience as a Corporate Virtue"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文核心是「耐心地等待长期回报」，financial impatience 与 children who pick the plums out of their pudding to eat them at once 都在批「急着拿」——倡导的恰恰是 patience 而不是 decisiveness。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 any guaranteed bonus of top executives could be delayed 10 years if their banks are under investigation for wrongdoing 是要约束银行家，作者立场也是支持约束，根本没有为银行家「挫败感」代言。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-2","kind":"reading","title":"Text 2","textNo":2,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 2","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-1","text":"Grade inflation – the gradual increase in average GPAs (grade-point averages) over the past few decades – is often considered a product of a consumer era in higher education, in which students are treated like customers to be pleased. But another, related force – a policy often buried deep in course catalogs called \"grade forgiveness\" – is helping raise GPAs."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-2","text":"Grade forgiveness allows students to retake a course in which they received a low grade, and the most recent grade or the highest grade is the only one that counts in calculating a student's overall GPA."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-3","text":"The use of this little-known practice has accelerated in recent years, as colleges continue to do their utmost to keep students in school (and paying tuition) and improve their graduation rates. When this practice first started decades ago, it was usually limited to freshmen, to give them a second chance to take a class in their first year if they struggled in their transition to college-level courses. But now most colleges, save for many selective campuses, allow all undergraduates, and even graduate students, to get their low grades forgiven."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-4","text":"College officials tend to emphasize that the goal of grade forgiveness is less about the grade itself and more about encouraging students to retake courses critical to their degree program and graduation without incurring a big penalty. \"Ultimately,\" said Jack Miner, Ohio State University's registrar, \"we see students achieve more success because they retake a course and do better in subsequent courses or master the content that allows them to graduate on time.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-5","text":"That said, there is a way in which grade forgiveness satisfies colleges' own needs as well. For public institutions, state funds are sometimes tied partly to their success on metrics such as graduation rates and student retention – so better grades can, by boosting figures like those, mean more money. And anything that raises GPAs will likely make students – who, at the end of the day, are paying the bill – feel they've gotten a better value for their tuition dollars, which is another big concern for colleges."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-6","text":"Indeed, grade forgiveness is just another way that universities are responding to consumers' expectations for higher education. Since students and parents expect a college degree to lead to a job, it is in the best interest of a school to turn out graduates who are as qualified as possible – or at least appear to be. On this, students' and colleges' incentives seem to be aligned."}]},"questions":[{"n":26,"stem":"What is commonly regarded as the cause of grade inflation?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The change of course catalogs."},{"key":"B","text":"Students' indifference to GPAs."},{"key":"C","text":"Colleges' neglect of GPAs."},{"key":"D","text":"The influence of consumer culture."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"什么通常被认为是分数膨胀的原因?","choicesZh":{"A":"课程目录的变化。","B":"学生对GPA的漠不关心。","C":"大学对GPA的忽视。","D":"消费文化的影响。"},"analysis":{"type":"What is commonly regarded as the cause of grade inflation? 直接问“被普遍认为的原因”，答案应能在原文一句话里直接读出，不需要跨段综合或推理。","parse":"主干是 What is commonly regarded as the cause，be regarded as 是“被视为/被认为是”的固定搭配，commonly 修饰“普遍程度”；of grade inflation 是 cause 的后置定语。无陷阱，但注意问的是“被普遍认为”的原因，不是作者自己的看法。","locateEn":"Grade inflation – the gradual increase in average GPAs (grade-point averages) over the past few decades – is often considered a product of a consumer era in higher education, in which students are treated like customers to be pleased.","locateZh":"“成绩通胀”——过去几十年间平均 GPA 的逐步上升——常被认为是高等教育消费时代的产物，在这个时代学生被当成需要取悦的顾客。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文只在 a policy often buried deep in course catalogs called \"grade forgiveness\" 里提到 course catalogs 是 grade forgiveness 这条政策的“藏身处”，从未说 catalogs 本身的变化导致了 grade inflation。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"全文只说学生被当作 customers to be pleased，没有任何一句描写学生 indifference to GPAs；相反，从“学生付费、看重文凭含金量”的语境看，学生反而在意 GPA。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文未出现“大学忽视 GPA”的论断；相反，下文 better grades can ... mean more money 表明学校非常重视 GPA，C 项与原文倾向直接相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判“归因消费时代”与 The influence of consumer culture 完全对应，且无超出原文的信息，是唯一与定位句等值的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"a product of a consumer era in higher education","opt":"The influence of consumer culture"}]}}}}},{"n":27,"stem":"What was the original purpose of grade forgiveness?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"To help freshmen adapt to college learning."},{"key":"B","text":"To maintain colleges' graduation rates."},{"key":"C","text":"To prepare graduates for a challenging future."},{"key":"D","text":"To increase universities' income from tuition."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"成绩宽恕的最初目的是什么?","choicesZh":{"A":"帮助新生适应大学学习。","B":"维持大学的毕业率。","C":"为毕业生应对充满挑战的未来做准备。","D":"增加大学的学费收入。"},"analysis":{"type":"What was the original purpose of grade forgiveness? 问的是过去某事实，原文必有对应叙述句，属于客观事实细节。","parse":"What was the original purpose of grade forgiveness 是一个简单系表问句，original 强调“最初的”而非“现在的”，是本题的关键限制——后文若有“现在的目的”作对比，必是干扰项温床。","locateEn":"When this practice first started decades ago, it was usually limited to freshmen, to give them a second chance to take a class in their first year if they struggled in their transition to college-level courses.","locateZh":"几十年前这一做法刚开始时，通常只面向大一新生，目的是在他们适应大学课程遇到困难时给他们第二次重修该课的机会。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判中“帮新生过渡大学课程”与 To help freshmen adapt to college learning 字面同义、主体（freshmen）一致、目的（adapt to college learning）等值，即为正解。","pairs":[{"orig":"to give them a second chance ... if they struggled in their transition to college-level courses","opt":"To help freshmen adapt to college learning"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"提高/维持毕业率是文中 as colleges continue to do their utmost to keep students in school (and paying tuition) and improve their graduation rates 描述的“近年加速使用”的原因，而非 first started 时的初衷；混淆“时代”导致全错。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"全文谈的都是“当下重修课程、按时毕业”，没有任何一句涉及 prepare graduates for a challenging future 的语义；纯凭常识猜的高频陷阱。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"P3 括号里的 (and paying tuition) 只是顺带提及学校希望学生留下继续交学费，是“近年加速使用”这一现状的副产品，并非政策最初成立的目的。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":28,"stem":"According to Paragraph 5, grade forgiveness enables colleges to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"obtain more financial support."},{"key":"B","text":"boost their student enrollments."},{"key":"C","text":"improve their teaching quality."},{"key":"D","text":"meet local governments' needs."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第5段,成绩宽恕使大学能够____","choicesZh":{"A":"获得更多财政支持。","B":"提高学生入学人数。","C":"改善教学质量。","D":"满足地方政府的需求。"},"analysis":{"type":"According to Paragraph 5, grade forgiveness enables colleges to____ 是带 According to 段内补全句，标准细节题，答案就在该段一两句之内。","parse":"主干 grade forgiveness enables colleges to ___，enable sb to do 是“使某人能够做”的固定搭配，空格处填动词原形短语；注意空格主语是 colleges，结果必须是“学校得到的好处”。","locateEn":"For public institutions, state funds are sometimes tied partly to their success on metrics such as graduation rates and student retention – so better grades can, by boosting figures like those, mean more money.","locateZh":"对公立院校而言，州政府拨款有时部分挂钩于毕业率、学生留校率等指标——所以更高的成绩能通过抬升这些数字，意味着更多的经费。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判“拿到更多政府拨款”与 obtain more financial support 完全对位；该选项把 state funds 抽象为 financial support，是教科书式的同义替换。","pairs":[{"orig":"state funds ... mean more money","opt":"obtain more financial support"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 student retention 指“学生留校率/续读率”，针对的是已入校的学生；选项 boost their student enrollments 指“增加新生招生人数”，二者是完全不同的指标。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"P5 通篇讲的是“分数变好→数字变好→钱变多/觉得值”，全程未涉及 teaching quality 是否提升；这是凭“学校得好处”常识联想出的伪关联。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文是“学校通过提分来满足州政府的拨款指标，从而拿到钱”——隐含的需求方是学校自己；选项把它说成“满足当地政府的需求”，把受益方颠倒成了政府。state ≠ local，外延也偷换。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":29,"stem":"What does the phrase \"to be aligned\" (Line 5, Para. 6) most probably mean?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"To counterbalance each other."},{"key":"B","text":"To complement each other."},{"key":"C","text":"To be identical with each other."},{"key":"D","text":"To be contradictory to each other."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"短语\"to be aligned\"(第6段第5行)最可能是什么意思?","choicesZh":{"A":"相互平衡。","B":"相互补充。","C":"彼此相同。","D":"相互矛盾。"},"analysis":{"type":"What does the phrase \"to be aligned\" ... most probably mean? 问的是某短语在原文中的含义，定义即词义题。","parse":"What does the phrase X most probably mean？mean 的宾语是疑问词 What，most probably 是“最可能”而非“一定”——它提示答案的依据是语境推论，所以备选项要在语境中找最契合的，而不是字典里最常见的。","locateEn":"Since students and parents expect a college degree to lead to a job, it is in the best interest of a school to turn out graduates who are as qualified as possible – or at least appear to be. On this, students' and colleges' incentives seem to be aligned.","locateZh":"既然学生和家长都指望一张大学文凭能带来一份工作，那么尽可能多产出“合格的”——或者至少看上去合格的——毕业生就符合学校的最大利益。在这一点上，学生和学校的动机似乎是一致的。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"counterbalance（抵消、平衡）意味着双方力量相反、相互制衡；而 Since students and parents expect a college degree to lead to a job, it is in the best interest of a school to turn out graduates ... 明确双方目标同向，不是相互抵消。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"complement 强调“双方互相补足缺失”——A 缺什么 B 给什么；原文却是双方都想要同一个东西（合格毕业生），不是互补关系，而是同向关系。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判“双方目标一致”与 To be identical with each other 同义；其余选项中只有 C 表“相同/一致”的语义。","pairs":[{"orig":"students' and colleges' incentives seem to be aligned","opt":"To be identical with each other"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"contradictory（自相矛盾、相互冲突）与 students' and colleges' incentives seem to be aligned 的语境正相反；选项把“激励一致”直接读成“激励矛盾”，方向倒置。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":30,"stem":"The author examines the practice of grade forgiveness by____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"assessing its feasibility."},{"key":"B","text":"analyzing the causes behind it."},{"key":"C","text":"comparing different views on it."},{"key":"D","text":"listing its long-run effects."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"作者通过____来审视成绩宽恕的做法","choicesZh":{"A":"评估其可行性。","B":"分析其背后的原因。","C":"比较对其不同的观点。","D":"列举其长期影响。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author examines the practice of grade forgiveness by____ 问全文的写作手法，必须聚合各段功能才能作答，归为主旨/手法题。","parse":"The author examines the practice of grade forgiveness by ___，examines = 考察/审视，by 短语作方式状语；空格补完后整句意思是“作者通过 ___ 的方式来考察这一做法”，所以选项一定是“一种考察手法”。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"feasibility（可行性）需要讨论“能不能做、有什么条件、可否推广”；全文谈的是“为什么会有这种做法”而非“能否实施”——grade forgiveness 早已在执行，根本不存在可行性讨论。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判“层层挖掘背后原因”与 analyzing the causes behind it 完全对位，is 用 analyzing 这一元动词概括了 P1→P5→P6 一脉相承的归因动作。","pairs":[{"orig":"For public institutions, state funds are sometimes tied partly to their success on metrics such as graduation rates and student retention – so better grades can, by boosting figures like those, mean more money","opt":"analyzing the causes behind it"},{"orig":"grade forgiveness is just another way that universities are responding to consumers' expectations for higher education","opt":"analyzing the causes behind it"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"compare different views 要求文中至少存在两个明确对立的观点被并列对照评估；P4 的“官方说法”与 P5 的“学校真实利益”虽有 That said 的轻转折，但作者并未把它们并列权衡，而是用前者作铺垫去引出后者——这是“挖原因”的递进，不是“并列对比观点”。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"long-run effects 要求作者列出该做法对教育/就业/社会等的长期后果；全文除了 P6 末句一笔带过“产出合格毕业生”外，根本未展开任何长期影响，只是反复在拆原因。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-3","kind":"reading","title":"Text 3","textNo":3,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 3","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-1","text":"This year marks exactly two centuries since the publication of \"Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus,\" by Mary Shelley. Even before the invention of the electric light bulb, the author produced a remarkable work of speculative fiction that would foreshadow many ethical questions to be raised by technologies yet to come."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-2","text":"Today the rapid growth of artificial intelligence (AI) raises fundamental questions: \"What is intelligence, identity, or consciousness? What makes humans humans?\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-3","text":"What is being called artificial general intelligence, machines that would imitate the way humans think, continues to evade scientists. Yet humans remain fascinated by the idea of robots that would look, move, and respond like humans, similar to those recently depicted on popular sci-fi TV series such as \"Westworld\" and \"Humans.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-4","text":"Just how people think is still far too complex to be understood, let alone reproduced, says David Eagleman, a Stanford University neuroscientist. \"We are just in a situation where there are no good theories explaining what consciousness actually is and how you could ever build a machine to get there.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-5","text":"But that doesn't mean crucial ethical issues involving AI aren't at hand. The coming use of autonomous vehicles, for example, poses thorny ethical questions. Human drivers sometimes must make split-second decisions. Their reactions may be a complex combination of instant reflexes, input from past driving experiences, and what their eyes and ears tell them in that moment. AI \"vision\" today is not nearly as sophisticated as that of humans. And to anticipate every imaginable driving situation is a difficult programming problem."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-6","text":"Whenever decisions are based on masses of data, \"you quickly get into a lot of ethical questions,\" notes Tan Kiat How, chief executive of a Singapore-based agency that is helping the government develop a voluntary code for the ethical use of AI. Along with Singapore, other governments and mega-corporations are beginning to establish their own guidelines. Britain is setting up a data ethics center. India released its AI ethics strategy this spring."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-7","text":"On June 7 Google pledged not to \"design or deploy AI\" that would cause \"overall harm,\" or to develop AI-directed weapons or use AI for surveillance that would violate international norms. It also pledged not to deploy AI whose use would violate international laws or human rights."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-8","text":"While the statement is vague, it represents one starting point. So does the idea that decisions made by AI systems should be explainable, transparent, and fair."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-9","text":"To put it another way: How can we make sure that the thinking of intelligent machines reflects humanity's highest values? Only then will they be useful servants and not Frankenstein's out-of-control monster."}]},"questions":[{"n":31,"stem":"Mary Shelley's novel Frankenstein is mentioned because it____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"fascinates AI scientists all over the world."},{"key":"B","text":"has remained popular for as long as 200 years."},{"key":"C","text":"involves some concerns raised by AI today."},{"key":"D","text":"has sparked serious ethical controversies."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"玛丽·雪莱的小说《弗兰肯斯坦》被提及是因为它____","choicesZh":{"A":"使全世界的人工智能科学家着迷。","B":"已经流行了长达200年之久。","C":"涉及当今人工智能引发的一些担忧。","D":"引发了严重的伦理争议。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 Mary Shelley's novel Frankenstein is mentioned because it____ 问的是作者「为何提到」这部小说，而不是小说本身的内容，落点在「举例的目的」上。","parse":"主干是 Mary Shelley's novel Frankenstein is mentioned，被动语态；省略的施事是作者；because 引导原因状语从句，从句主语 it 回指 Frankenstein。空格要补的是 because 之后的整个谓语，因此选项都以动词原形开头。结构直白，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Even before the invention of the electric light bulb, the author produced a remarkable work of speculative fiction that would foreshadow many ethical questions to be raised by technologies yet to come.","locateZh":"甚至在电灯发明之前，作者就写出了一部杰出的思辨小说，预示了后世技术将引发的诸多伦理问题。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未说这部小说 fascinates AI scientists——感到痴迷的对象在 P3 是「humans」（普通人对类人机器人着迷），且对象是机器人不是小说本身。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 two centuries since the publication 只是交代年代背景，是「为什么今年提它」的时机说明，不是「为什么提它」的目的；题干问的是 because it 后面的论证目的，不是出版年龄。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"C 项把 P1 论点句「预示后世技术的伦理问题」直接收窄并平移到「涉及今日 AI 引发的关切」，与预判一致；选 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"foreshadow many ethical questions to be raised by technologies yet to come","opt":"involves some concerns raised by AI today"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文只说小说 foreshadow many ethical questions——「预示」伦理问题，并未说它 has sparked serious ethical controversies（引发了严重的伦理争议）。从「预示」到「引发严重争议」跨了两步推理，超出文本支持范围。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":32,"stem":"In David Eagleman's opinion, our current knowledge of consciousness____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"helps explain artificial intelligence."},{"key":"B","text":"can be misleading to robot making."},{"key":"C","text":"inspires popular sci-fi TV series."},{"key":"D","text":"is too limited for us to reproduce it."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"在David Eagleman看来,我们目前对意识的认知____","choicesZh":{"A":"有助于解释人工智能。","B":"可能对机器人制造产生误导。","C":"启发了流行的科幻电视剧。","D":"太有限以至于我们无法复制它。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 In David Eagleman's opinion, our current knowledge of consciousness____ 要求复述某人对某事的客观判断，不涉及超出原文的推理或态度。","parse":"In David Eagleman's opinion 是介词短语作状语，限定观点归属；主语是 our current knowledge of consciousness，of 短语后置修饰 knowledge；空格补谓语动词。注意主语是「关于意识的认知现状」，不是「意识本身」，主语理解一旦走偏整题就废。","locateEn":"\"We are just in a situation where there are no good theories explaining what consciousness actually is and how you could ever build a machine to get there.\"","locateZh":"「我们的处境就是：目前根本没有像样的理论可以解释意识到底是什么、以及人怎么造出一台机器去抵达意识。」","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Eagleman 强调「没有好理论」，意即「对 AI 没什么解释力」；A 却说现有认知 helps explain artificial intelligence，方向恰好相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未说现有认知会 be misleading to robot making——它只说「认知不足」，从未给出「会误导」的判断。从「不足」到「会误导」是新增的语义。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"P3 提到 popular sci-fi TV series such as \"Westworld\" and \"Humans.\" 是「人类对类人机器人的迷恋」促成了科幻剧的流行，做主语的是「人类的迷恋」，不是「Eagleman 的意识认知」；选项把功劳错记到 Eagleman 的意识知识头上。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"D 项把 P4 引语的「没好理论＋造不出机器」浓缩成「认知太有限以致无法复制」，与预判一致；选 D。","pairs":[{"orig":"no good theories explaining what consciousness actually is and how you could ever build a machine to get there","opt":"is too limited for us to reproduce it"}]}}}}},{"n":33,"stem":"The solution to the ethical issues brought by autonomous vehicles____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"can hardly ever be found."},{"key":"B","text":"is still beyond our capacity."},{"key":"C","text":"causes little public concern."},{"key":"D","text":"has aroused much curiosity."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"自动驾驶车辆带来的伦理问题的解决方案____","choicesZh":{"A":"几乎不可能找到。","B":"仍然超出我们的能力范围。","C":"很少引起公众关注。","D":"引起了很大的好奇心。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The solution to the ethical issues brought by autonomous vehicles____ 问的是「这些伦理问题的解决方案」目前处于何种状态，答案应在原文有直接交代的文字依据。","parse":"主语 The solution 后跟两个层层后置的修饰：to the ethical issues（解决谁的问题）+ brought by autonomous vehicles（这些问题是谁带来的，brought 是过去分词作定语）。主干就是「The solution ____」。基础薄弱考生易把 brought by autonomous vehicles 误读为主句谓语，把题干读成「自动驾驶带来了解决方案」，意思全反。","locateEn":"AI \"vision\" today is not nearly as sophisticated as that of humans. And to anticipate every imaginable driving situation is a difficult programming problem.","locateZh":"今日 AI 的「视觉」远不及人类。要为每一种可以想象到的驾驶情景做预案，是一个困难的编程问题。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文只说当下技术「难」，没说 can hardly ever be found（几乎永远找不到解决方案）。把「难」升级成「几乎永远不可能」，时间从 today 扩展到 ever，是越界推断。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"B 同时含「still（眼下尚未）」与「beyond our capacity（能力之外）」两层关键语义，与预判完全吻合；选 B。","pairs":[{"orig":"AI \"vision\" today is not nearly as sophisticated as that of humans. And to anticipate every imaginable driving situation is a difficult programming problem.","opt":"is still beyond our capacity"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"autonomous vehicles, for example, poses thorny ethical questions 明确说这是「棘手的伦理问题」，本身就是公共关切，选项却说 causes little public concern——「几乎没有公众关注」，方向相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未提及公众对自动驾驶 aroused much curiosity（激起强烈好奇心）——文章谈的是「难度／伦理」，curiosity 这层情绪从未出现。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":34,"stem":"The author's attitude toward Google's pledges is one of____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"affirmation."},{"key":"B","text":"skepticism."},{"key":"C","text":"contempt."},{"key":"D","text":"respect."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"态度题","stemZh":"作者对Google的承诺的态度是____","choicesZh":{"A":"肯定的。","B":"怀疑的。","C":"轻蔑的。","D":"尊重的。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The author's attitude toward Google's pledges is one of____，attitude 直接点明考查态度，选项是 affirmation / skepticism / contempt / respect 四个态度名词。","parse":"句型 The author's attitude toward Google's pledges is one of____，one of 后接表态度的抽象名词；toward 引导态度对象。空格只能填名词。常见错读是把 attitude 和 pledge 搞混主体——题干问的是「作者对谷歌承诺的态度」，不是「谷歌对 AI 的态度」。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"While 的让步结构把不足前置交代，主句给的是肯定评价；预判极性「正面」唯一匹配的就是 A，选 A。","pairs":[{"orig":"it represents one starting point","opt":"affirmation"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"skepticism（怀疑）需要原文出现 doubt / question / unclear whether 等怀疑信号；而 P8 用的是 starting point 这种正面定位，且 P9 顺势升华到「让 AI 反映人类最高价值」，作者全程在推进而非质疑。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"contempt（鄙夷／轻蔑）是极强的负面情绪，需要原文出现 ridiculous / absurd / shameful 等蔑视性词汇；P8 全无此类用词，反而用 starting point 给予正面认可。把「指出含糊」放大到「鄙夷」是情绪强度的越级。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"respect（敬意／尊重）的强度高于 affirmation——它需要原文出现 admire / commend / praise 等明确表彰用词；作者只说 starting point（起点），并用 vague 让步承认其不足，远谈不上「敬重」。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":35,"stem":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"AI's Future: In the Hands of Tech Giants"},{"key":"B","text":"Frankenstein, the Novel Predicting the Age of AI"},{"key":"C","text":"The Conscience of AI: Complex But Inevitable"},{"key":"D","text":"AI Shall Be Killers Once Out of Control"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"以下哪项是这篇文章的最佳标题?","choicesZh":{"A":"人工智能的未来:掌握在科技巨头手中","B":"《弗兰肯斯坦》,预言人工智能时代的小说","C":"人工智能的良知:复杂但不可避免","D":"人工智能一旦失控就会成为杀手"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 Which of the following would be the best title for the text? 是典型的标题选择题，要求选项覆盖全文中心。","parse":"标准疑问句 Which of the following would be the best title for the text?，would be 是虚拟语气表「假定挑选」，无理解负担。选项是四个名词短语，结构平铺，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"In the Hands of Tech Giants（在科技巨头手中）只覆盖 P6 P7 列举 Google、新加坡等机构动作的两段，把它当成全文主旨就漏掉 P1 的 Frankenstein、P4 的意识难解、P5 的自动驾驶。且原文从未断言 AI 未来「就在巨头手里」。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Frankenstein, the Novel Predicting the Age of AI 只截取首段的引子。Frankenstein 在本文是修辞性引子，而非主角；标题里以小说为主体就违反了「举例段不进主旨」的原则。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"C 是四个选项中唯一同时收住「良知（话题）」「复杂（难度）」「不可避免（立场）」三个主线要素的标题；选 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"How can we make sure that the thinking of intelligent machines reflects humanity's highest values? Only then will they be useful servants and not Frankenstein's out-of-control monster.","opt":"The Conscience of AI: Complex But Inevitable"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"AI Shall Be Killers Once Out of Control（AI 一旦失控就会成为杀手）把 Frankenstein's out-of-control monster 的比喻字面化为「杀手」，并用 Shall 表强烈预言。原文是「失控会成为像科学怪人那样的怪物」，主旨是「我们要避免它」，不是「它必然成为杀手」。情绪强度和语义指向都超纲。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-4","kind":"reading","title":"Text 4","textNo":4,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 4","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-1","text":"States will be able to force more people to pay sales tax when they make online purchases under a Supreme Court decision Thursday that will leave shoppers with lighter wallets but is a big financial win for states."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-2","text":"The Supreme Court's opinion Thursday overruled a pair of decades-old decisions that states said cost them billions of dollars in lost revenue annually. The decisions made it more difficult for states to collect sales tax on certain online purchases."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-3","text":"The cases the court overturned said that if a business was shipping a customer's purchase to a state where the business didn't have a physical presence such as a warehouse or office, the business didn't have to collect sales tax for the state. Customers were generally responsible for paying the sales tax to the state themselves if they weren't charged it, but most didn't realize they owed it and few paid."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-4","text":"Justice Anthony Kennedy wrote that the previous decisions were flawed. \"Each year the physical presence rule becomes further removed from economic reality and results in significant revenue losses to the States,\" he wrote in an opinion joined by four other justices. Kennedy wrote that the rule \"limited states' ability to seek long-term prosperity and has prevented market participants from competing on an even playing field.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-5","text":"The ruling is a victory for big chains with a presence in many states, since they usually collect sales tax on online purchases already. Now, rivals will be charging sales tax where they hadn't before. Big chains have been collecting sales tax nationwide because they typically have physical stores in whatever state a purchase is being shipped to. Amazon.com, with its network of warehouses, also collects sales tax in every state that charges it, though third-party sellers who use the site don't have to."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-6","text":"Until now, many sellers that have a physical presence in only a single state or a few states have been able to avoid charging sales taxes when they ship to addresses outside those states. Sellers that use eBay and Etsy, which provide platforms for smaller sellers, also haven't been collecting sales tax nationwide. Under the ruling Thursday, states can pass laws requiring out-of-state sellers to collect the state's sales tax from customers and send it to the state."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-7","text":"Retail trade groups praised the ruling, saying it levels the playing field for local and online businesses. The losers, said retail analyst Neil Saunders, are online-only retailers, especially smaller ones. Those retailers may face headaches complying with various state sales tax laws. The Small Business & Entrepreneurship Council advocacy group said in a statement, \"Small businesses and internet entrepreneurs are not well served at all by this decision.\""}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"The Supreme Court decision Thursday will____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"better businesses' relations with states."},{"key":"B","text":"put most online businesses in a dilemma."},{"key":"C","text":"make more online shoppers pay sales tax."},{"key":"D","text":"force some states to cut sales tax."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"最高法院周四的裁决将____","choicesZh":{"A":"改善企业与各州的关系。","B":"使大多数在线业务陷入困境。","C":"让更多网购者缴纳销售税。","D":"迫使一些州削减销售税。"},"analysis":{"type":"The Supreme Court decision Thursday will____ 是不完整陈述句，让选项做谓语补完判决的直接结果，无 infer/imply 字样。","parse":"Q36 是一个空缺谓语的陈述句：主语 The Supreme Court decision Thursday，情态动词 will 之后由选项补完动词词组。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"States will be able to force more people to pay sales tax when they make online purchases under a Supreme Court decision Thursday that will leave shoppers with lighter wallets but is a big financial win for states.","locateZh":"根据周四最高法院的一项判决，各州将能强制更多人在网购时缴纳销售税；该判决会让购物者钱包变瘦，但对各州而言是一场重大的财政胜利。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"better businesses' relations with states 即改善企业与各州的关系，原文从未讨论企业-州关系是否好转。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"put most online businesses in a dilemma 说大多数网店陷入两难，原文只点名小型线上零售商 online-only retailers, especially smaller ones 会有麻烦，远未涉及 most。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"make more online shoppers pay sales tax 是定位句前半段的纯粹同义改写，与预判完全吻合，故选 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"States will be able to force more people to pay sales tax when they make online purchases","opt":"make more online shoppers pay sales tax"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"force some states to cut sales tax 让各州减税与原文 a big financial win for states 让各州得利的方向相反，判决是帮各州收税而非减税。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":37,"stem":"It can be learned from paragraphs 2 and 3 that the overruled decisions____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"have led to the dominance of e-commerce."},{"key":"B","text":"have cost consumers a lot over the years."},{"key":"C","text":"were widely criticized by online purchasers."},{"key":"D","text":"were considered unfavorable by states."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"从第2段和第3段可以得知,被推翻的裁决____","choicesZh":{"A":"导致了电子商务的主导地位。","B":"多年来使消费者付出了很大代价。","C":"受到网购者的广泛批评。","D":"被各州认为是不利的。"},"analysis":{"type":"It can be learned from paragraphs 2 and 3 that 表面像推断，实则限定在两段内做事实归纳，无需跨段推理。","parse":"形式主语 it 句 It can be learned from paragraphs 2 and 3 that the overruled decisions____，that 从句缺谓语由选项补完。无陷阱。","locateEn":"The Supreme Court's opinion Thursday overruled a pair of decades-old decisions that states said cost them billions of dollars in lost revenue annually.","locateZh":"周四最高法院的意见推翻了一对存在数十年的旧判决——各州称这些旧判决每年让它们损失数十亿美元的税收。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"have led to the dominance of e-commerce 把旧判决说成促成电商主导地位，第 2、3 段从未讨论电商是否占据主导。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"have cost consumers a lot over the years 把受损方说成 consumers 消费者，原文里旧判决 cost them billions of dollars 的 them 指代 states 各州，受损方是州而非消费者。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"were widely criticized by online purchasers 说旧判决被网购者广泛批评，第 3 段只说大多数消费者 didn't realize they owed it and few paid——他们对税并不知情，更谈不上批评。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"were considered unfavorable by states 在评价主体（states）与极性（unfavorable）上都与预判吻合，故选 D。","pairs":[{"orig":"states said cost them billions of dollars in lost revenue annually","opt":"were considered unfavorable by states"}]}}}}},{"n":38,"stem":"According to Justice Anthony Kennedy, the physical presence rule has____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"hindered economic development."},{"key":"B","text":"brought prosperity to the country."},{"key":"C","text":"harmed fair market competition."},{"key":"D","text":"boosted growth in states' revenue."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据大法官Anthony Kennedy的说法,实体存在规则____","choicesZh":{"A":"阻碍了经济发展。","B":"为国家带来了繁荣。","C":"损害了公平的市场竞争。","D":"促进了各州财政收入的增长。"},"analysis":{"type":"According to Justice Anthony Kennedy, the physical presence rule has____ 问 Kennedy 对 rule 的明确表态，是原文直引的观点细节。","parse":"Q38 主干为 the physical presence rule has + 选项补完的过去分词短语；状语 According to Justice Anthony Kennedy 指明观点归属。结构清晰。","locateEn":"Kennedy wrote that the rule \"limited states' ability to seek long-term prosperity and has prevented market participants from competing on an even playing field.\"","locateZh":"Kennedy 写道，该规则限制了各州追求长期繁荣的能力，并阻止了市场参与者在公平的竞争环境中角逐。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"hindered economic development 说阻碍经济发展，原文只提到 significant revenue losses to the States 各州税收损失与 long-term prosperity 长期繁荣，是州层面的财政问题，未上升到国家经济发展层面。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"brought prosperity to the country 说该规则给国家带来繁荣，与 Kennedy limited states' ability to seek long-term prosperity 限制各州追求繁荣 的判断完全相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"动作 prevented...from competing → harmed，对象 market participants ... on an even playing field → fair market competition，预判核心要素全部对上，故选 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"has prevented market participants from competing on an even playing field","opt":"harmed fair market competition"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"boosted growth in states' revenue 说促进各州税收增长，与 significant revenue losses to the States 让各州税收大幅减少 完全相反。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":39,"stem":"Who are most likely to welcome the Supreme Court ruling?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Internet entrepreneurs."},{"key":"B","text":"Big-chain owners."},{"key":"C","text":"Third-party sellers."},{"key":"D","text":"Small retailers."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"谁最有可能欢迎最高法院的裁决?","choicesZh":{"A":"互联网企业家。","B":"大型连锁店所有者。","C":"第三方卖家。","D":"小型零售商。"},"analysis":{"type":"Who are most likely to welcome the Supreme Court ruling? 是直接问受益群体的疑问句，答案来自原文中明确点名为受益方的句子。","parse":"Q39 是特殊疑问句，主语 Who，谓语 are，most likely 为程度修饰，to welcome 不定式作表语补足语；选项是名词作主语补完。","locateEn":"The ruling is a victory for big chains with a presence in many states, since they usually collect sales tax on online purchases already.","locateZh":"这项判决是大型连锁企业的一场胜利，因为这些在多州都有实体的连锁本来就大多已经在为网购代收销售税。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Internet entrepreneurs 互联网创业者，对应原文尾段 Small businesses and internet entrepreneurs are not well served at all by this decision——他们恰恰是反对方，不会 welcome。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Big-chain owners 与定位句 victory for big chains 在受益对象上完全一致，与预判吻合，故选 B。","pairs":[{"orig":"a victory for big chains with a presence in many states","opt":"Big-chain owners"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Third-party sellers 是第三方卖家。原文 third-party sellers who use the site don't have to 只说他们此前不必代收税，并未对其将欢迎或反对新判决作任何表态。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Small retailers 小型零售商，对应 The losers, said retail analyst Neil Saunders, are online-only retailers, especially smaller ones——他们正是判决的输家，更不可能欢迎。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":40,"stem":"In dealing with the Supreme Court decision Thursday, the author____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"gives a factual account of it and discusses its consequences."},{"key":"B","text":"describes the long and complicated process of its making."},{"key":"C","text":"presents its main points with conflicting views on them."},{"key":"D","text":"cites some cases related to it and analyzes their implications."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"在处理最高法院周四的裁决时,作者____","choicesZh":{"A":"对其进行事实性陈述并讨论其后果。","B":"描述了其制定的漫长而复杂的过程。","C":"陈述了其要点以及对这些要点相互冲突的观点。","D":"引用了与之相关的一些案例并分析了它们的含义。"},"analysis":{"type":"In dealing with the Supreme Court decision Thursday, the author____ 问的是作者整体如何呈现该事件，必须串读全文才能作答。","parse":"Q40 由状语 In dealing with the Supreme Court decision Thursday 引导，主句 the author____ 缺谓语由选项补完。状语提示讨论对象，主句留空动词。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"gives a factual account of it and discusses its consequences 中两个动作 give a factual account / discuss consequences 正好对应作者前后两段使命，与预判完全吻合，故选 A。","pairs":[{"orig":"States will be able to force more people to pay sales tax when they make online purchases under a Supreme Court decision Thursday","opt":"gives a factual account of it"},{"orig":"Retail trade groups praised the ruling","opt":"discusses its consequences"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"describes the long and complicated process of its making 说作者描写判决的漫长复杂制定过程，全文从未叙述法院如何审理、辩论、表决等任何过程性细节。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"presents its main points with conflicting views on them 说作者陈列判决要点并附冲突观点。全文确有不同反应（连锁称赞、小卖家反对），但作者并未列出判决的 main points 要点清单，只引了 Kennedy 单人观点；conflicting views 描述也偏强，原文是利益分析而非观点对撞。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"cites some cases related to it and analyzes their implications 说作者引用相关案例并分析其含义。文中只在第 3 段提到 The cases the court overturned 这一被推翻的旧判决，但作者并未独立列举多个相关案例来讨论，重心是新判决本身的影响，不是案例分析。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-b","kind":"ordering","title":"Section II Part B 段落排序","directions":"Directions: The following paragraphs are given in a wrong order. For questions 41 -45, you are required to reorganize these paragraphs into a coherent text by choosing from the list A-G and filling them into the numbered boxes. Paragraphs C and F have been correctly placed. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","points":10,"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-b-1","label":"A","text":"These tools can help you win every argument – not in the unhelpful sense of beating your opponents but in the better sense of learning about the issues that divide people. Learning why they disagree with us and learning to talk and work together with them. If we readjust our view of arguments – from a verbal fight or tennis game to a reasoned exchange through which we all gain mutual respect, and understanding – then we change the very nature of what it means to \"win\" an argument."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-2","label":"B","text":"Of course, many discussions are not so successful. Still, we need to be careful not to accuse opponents of bad arguments too quickly. We need to learn how to evaluate them properly. A large part of evaluation is calling out bad arguments, but we also need to admit good arguments by opponents and to apply the same critical standards to ourselves. Humility requires you to recognize weakness in your own arguments and sometimes also to accept reasons on the opposite side."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-3","label":"C","text":"None of these will be easy but you can start even if others refuse to. Next time you state your position, formulate an argument for what you claim and honestly ask yourself whether your argument is any good. Next time you talk with someone who takes a stand, ask them to give you a reason for their view. Spell out their argument fully and charitably. Assess its strength impartially. Raise objections and listen carefully to their replies."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-4","label":"D","text":"Carnegie would be right if arguments were fights, which is how we often think of them. Like physical fights, verbal fights can leave both sides bloodied. Even when you win, you end up no better off. Your prospects would be almost as dismal if arguments were even just competitions – like, say, tennis games. Pairs of opponents hit the ball back and forth until one winner emerges from all who entered. Everybody else loses. This kind of thinking is why so many people try to avoid arguments, especially about politics and religion."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-5","label":"E","text":"In his 1936 work How to Win Friends and Influence People, Dale Carnegie wrote: \"There is only one way…to get the best of an argument – and that is to avoid it.\" This aversion to arguments is common, but it depends on a mistaken view of arguments that causes profound problems for our personal and social lives – and in many ways misses the point of arguing in the first place."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-6","label":"F","text":"These views of arguments also undermine reason. If you see a conversation as a fight or competition, you can win by cheating as long as you don't get caught. You will be happy to convince people with bad arguments. You can call their views stupid, or joke about how ignorant they are. None of these tricks will help you understand them, their positions or the issues that divide you, but they can help you win – in one way."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-7","label":"G","text":"There is a better way to win arguments. Imagine that you favor increasing the minimum wage in our state, and I do not. If you yell, \"Yes,\" and I yell, \"No,\" neither of us learns anything. We neither understand nor respect each other, and we have no basis for compromise or cooperation. In contrast, suppose you give a reasonable argument: that full-time workers should not have to live in poverty. Then I counter with another reasonable argument: that a higher minimum wage will force businesses to employ fewer people for less time. Now we can understand each other's positions and recognize our shared values, since we both care about needy workers."}],"choices":[{"key":"A","text":"These tools can help you win every argument – not in the unhelpful sense of beating your opponents but in the better sense of learning about the issues that divide people. Learning why they disagree with us and learning to talk and work together with them. If we readjust our view of arguments – from a verbal fight or tennis game to a reasoned exchange through which we all gain mutual respect, and understanding – then we change the very nature of what it means to \"win\" an argument."},{"key":"B","text":"Of course, many discussions are not so successful. Still, we need to be careful not to accuse opponents of bad arguments too quickly. We need to learn how to evaluate them properly. A large part of evaluation is calling out bad arguments, but we also need to admit good arguments by opponents and to apply the same critical standards to ourselves. Humility requires you to recognize weakness in your own arguments and sometimes also to accept reasons on the opposite side."},{"key":"C","text":"None of these will be easy but you can start even if others refuse to. Next time you state your position, formulate an argument for what you claim and honestly ask yourself whether your argument is any good. Next time you talk with someone who takes a stand, ask them to give you a reason for their view. Spell out their argument fully and charitably. Assess its strength impartially. Raise objections and listen carefully to their replies."},{"key":"D","text":"Carnegie would be right if arguments were fights, which is how we often think of them. Like physical fights, verbal fights can leave both sides bloodied. Even when you win, you end up no better off. Your prospects would be almost as dismal if arguments were even just competitions – like, say, tennis games. Pairs of opponents hit the ball back and forth until one winner emerges from all who entered. Everybody else loses. This kind of thinking is why so many people try to avoid arguments, especially about politics and religion."},{"key":"E","text":"In his 1936 work How to Win Friends and Influence People, Dale Carnegie wrote: \"There is only one way…to get the best of an argument – and that is to avoid it.\" This aversion to arguments is common, but it depends on a mistaken view of arguments that causes profound problems for our personal and social lives – and in many ways misses the point of arguing in the first place."},{"key":"F","text":"These views of arguments also undermine reason. If you see a conversation as a fight or competition, you can win by cheating as long as you don't get caught. You will be happy to convince people with bad arguments. You can call their views stupid, or joke about how ignorant they are. None of these tricks will help you understand them, their positions or the issues that divide you, but they can help you win – in one way."},{"key":"G","text":"There is a better way to win arguments. Imagine that you favor increasing the minimum wage in our state, and I do not. If you yell, \"Yes,\" and I yell, \"No,\" neither of us learns anything. We neither understand nor respect each other, and we have no basis for compromise or cooperation. In contrast, suppose you give a reasonable argument: that full-time workers should not have to live in poverty. Then I counter with another reasonable argument: that a higher minimum wage will force businesses to employ fewer people for less time. Now we can understand each other's positions and recognize our shared values, since we both care about needy workers."}],"questions":[{"n":41,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"E"},{"n":42,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"D"},{"n":43,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"G"},{"n":44,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"B"},{"n":45,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"A"}],"order":["E","D","F","G","B","C","A"],"fixed":{"2":"F","5":"C"},"chain":"41. → 42. → F → 43. → 44. → C → 45.","orderChain":"正确顺序 41 E → 42 D → 预给 F → 43 G → 44 B → 预给 C → 45 A","seams":["【引出后一段】 段 D 词汇复现：E 引入、D 接着点名 。E 第一次完整介绍卡耐基，D 段首直接以 Carnegie 起句承接，专名复现把两段连成「提出—展开」链。","【承接前一段】 段 E 词汇复现：展开 E 留下的「错误观」 。E 结尾抛出「错误认识」，D 以 Carnegie 起句承接并具体化为「把争论当打架」，既复现专名又回答了 E 的悬念。","【引出后一段】 段 F 指代衔接：D 给「These views」备料 。D 把争论归为「打架/比赛」两类观点，正是后面给定段 F「These views」所指代的对象，因此 D 必须排在 F 之前、即 42 位。","【承接前一段】 段 F 逻辑连接词：坏方式→更好方式 。给定段 F 讲把争论当打架/比赛「败坏理性」的坏方式，G 段首「a better way」正是对它的转折对照，紧接其后构成「坏—好」对比。","【承接前一段】 段 G 逻辑连接词：对 G 成功例的让步 。G 给了一个成功讲理的例子，B 段首「Of course…Still」先承认「并非都成功」再转向「要谨慎评估」，是对 G 的让步承接。","【引出后一段】 段 C 指代衔接：B 列要求、C 的 None of these 回收 。B 列出评估、谦逊、自省等一组要求，给定段 C 段首「None of these」正回指这组要求，故 B 紧排在 C 之前、即 44 位。","【承接前一段】 段 C 指代衔接：These tools 指 C 的步骤 。给定段 C 列出一整套可照做的步骤（提出论据、索要理由、公正评估），A 段首「These tools」正回指这套步骤，故 A 紧接 C，作全文收束。"]},{"id":"section2-part-c","kind":"translation","title":"Section II Part C 英译汉","directions":"Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-c-1","text":"It was only after I started to write a weekly column about the medical journals, and began to read scientific papers from beginning to end, that I realised just how bad much of the medical literature frequently was. I came to recognise various signs of a bad paper: the kind of paper that purports to show that people who eat more than one kilo of broccoli a week were 1.17 times more likely than those who eat less to suffer late in life from pernicious anaemia. (46) There is a great deal of this kind of nonsense in the medical journals which, when taken up by broadcasters and the lay press, generates both health scares and short-lived dietary enthusiasms."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-2","text":"Why is so much bad science published? A recent paper, titled \"The Natural Selection of Bad Science\", published on the Royal Society's open science website, attempts to answer this intriguing and important question. It says that the problem is not merely that people do bad science, but that our current system of career advancement positively encourages it. What is important is not truth, but publication, which has become almost an end in itself. There has been a kind of inflationary process at work: (47) nowadays anyone applying for a research post has to have published twice the number of papers that would have been required for the same post only 10 years ago. Never mind the quality, then, count the number."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-3","text":"(48) Attempts have been made to curb this tendency, for example, by trying to incorporate some measure of quality as well as quantity into the assessment of an applicant's papers. This is the famed citation index, that is to say the number of times a paper has been quoted elsewhere in the scientific literature, the assumption being that an important paper will be cited more often than one of small account. (49) This would be reasonable if it were not for the fact that scientists can easily arrange to cite themselves in their future publications, or get associates to do so for them in return for similar favours."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-4","text":"Boiling down an individual's output to simple metrics, such as number of publications or journal impacts, entails considerable savings in time, energy and ambiguity. Unfortunately, the long-term costs of using simple quantitative metrics to assess researcher merit are likely to be quite great. (50) If we are serious about ensuring that our science is both meaningful and reproducible, we must ensure that our institutions encourage that kind of science."}]},"sentences":[{"n":46,"en":"There is a great deal of this kind of nonsense in the medical journals which, when taken up by broadcasters and the lay press, generates both health scares and short-lived dietary enthusiasms.","refZh":"医学期刊里充斥着大量这类无稽之谈，这些无稽之谈一旦被广播媒体和非专业的大众报刊拿去传播，就会既制造健康恐慌，又掀起一阵阵昙花一现的饮食热潮。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"There is a great deal of this kind of nonsense in the medical journals","lit":"医学期刊里有大量这类无稽之谈","role":"（主干（there be 句））","note":"难点： this kind of nonsense 回指 + nonsense 取义 this kind of nonsense 须按第一步还原成第 1 段那类伪相关结论 people who eat more than one kilo of broccoli a week were 1.17 times more likely...to suffer late in life from pernicious anaemia，译「这类无稽之谈／胡说」；nonsense 不是「废话」，在此是「毫无道理的伪科学结论」。"},{"en":"which, when taken up by broadcasters and the lay press,","lit":"这些无稽之谈，当被广播媒体和非专业大众媒体采纳时","role":"（which 定语从句 + 内嵌 when 时间状语）","note":"难点： which 指代 nonsense；taken up 与 lay press 取义 which 指代的是前面的 nonsense（这类伪科学），不是 journals——看后文「制造健康恐慌」可反推；take up 在此是「采纳、拿来传播」而非「拿起」；the lay press 的 lay 是熟词僻义「外行的、非专业的」，指面向大众的通俗媒体。"},{"en":"generates both health scares and short-lived dietary enthusiasms.","lit":"既制造健康恐慌，又制造昙花一现的饮食热潮","role":"（which 从句的谓语（与 generates 主体是 which=nonsense））","note":"难点： short-lived 与 dietary enthusiasms 具体化 short-lived 直译「短命的」不通，汉化为「昙花一现的、来去匆匆的」；dietary enthusiasms 抽象名词具体化为「（一阵阵的）饮食热／养生热」，比硬译「饮食热情」自然。"}]},{"n":47,"en":"nowadays anyone applying for a research post has to have published twice the number of papers that would have been required for the same post only 10 years ago.","refZh":"如今，任何申请研究职位的人，其发表的论文数量都必须达到仅仅 10 年前申请同一职位所要求篇数的两倍。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"nowadays anyone applying for a research post","lit":"如今任何申请研究职位的人","role":"（时间状语 + 主语（含 applying 现在分词后置定语））","note":"难点： applying for 现在分词作后置定语；post 取义 applying for a research post 是现在分词短语作后置定语，修饰 anyone，等于 anyone who applies for...，汉化时前移译成「申请研究职位的（任何人）」；post 是熟词僻义「职位、岗位」，不是「邮件／柱子」。"},{"en":"has to have published twice the number of papers","lit":"必须已经发表了两倍数量的论文","role":"（谓语（完成式不定式）+ 宾语）","note":"难点： twice the number of 倍数表达 twice the number of papers 是「论文数量的两倍」，即「两倍数量的论文」；has to have published 是「必须已经发表」（完成式强调到申请时已完成），不要漏掉「已经」这层完成意味。"},{"en":"that would have been required for the same post only 10 years ago.","lit":"（这一数量是）仅仅 10 年前申请同一职位本会被要求的","role":"（that 定语从句修饰 the number of papers（含虚拟语气））","note":"难点： 比较基准还原 + would have been required 虚拟 that 从句修饰 the number（论文数量），交代对照基准：把 twice 还原成完整比较——「是 10 年前申请同一职位所要求篇数的两倍」。would have been required 是对过去的虚拟「（当年）本会要求的」，译时点出「（10 年前）所要求的」即可，不必生硬带「本会」。"}]},{"n":48,"en":"Attempts have been made to curb this tendency, for example, by trying to incorporate some measure of quality as well as quantity into the assessment of an applicant's papers.","refZh":"人们已经做出种种努力来遏制这种倾向，例如，设法把对质量（而不只是数量）的某种衡量标准，纳入对申请者论文的评估当中。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Attempts have been made to curb this tendency,","lit":"（人们）已经做出种种努力来遏制这种倾向","role":"（主干（被动）+ to 不定式作目的）","note":"难点： 被动语态转主动 + this tendency 还原 + curb 取义 Attempts have been made 是被动，汉语宜转无主句／主动「（人们）已做出努力」；this tendency 按第一步回指第 2 段 Never mind the quality, then, count the number.，指「只数论文篇数、不顾质量」的倾向；curb 是「遏制、抑制」，非「路缘」。"},{"en":"for example, by trying to incorporate some measure of quality as well as quantity","lit":"例如，通过设法把某种对质量和数量的衡量都纳入","role":"（举例 + by 方式状语（incorporate...））","note":"难点： A as well as B 语序 + incorporate...into 搭配 + measure 取义 quality as well as quantity 重心在 quality，意为「不仅数量、还有质量」，译时把质量摆出来；incorporate A into B 是「把 A 纳入 B」固定搭配，into 的宾语在下一块；measure 此处是「衡量标准、尺度」，不是「措施」。"},{"en":"into the assessment of an applicant's papers.","lit":"纳入对申请者论文的评估之中","role":"（into 介词短语（incorporate 的去向））","note":"难点： assessment of...承接 incorporate into 这块是 incorporate...into 的落点，与上一块连读：把质量衡量「纳入对申请者论文的评估当中」。assessment 译「评估、考评」。"}]},{"n":49,"en":"This would be reasonable if it were not for the fact that scientists can easily arrange to cite themselves in their future publications, or get associates to do so for them in return for similar favours.","refZh":"如果不是因为存在这样一个事实，这种做法本来是合理的：科学家可以轻而易举地安排在自己日后的论文里引用自己，或者让同行替他们这样做，作为回报再给对方类似的好处。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"This would be reasonable if it were not for the fact that","lit":"要不是因为这样一个事实，这种做法本来是合理的","role":"（主句（虚拟）+ if it were not for 条件 + that 同位语引导）","note":"难点： if it were not for 虚拟句型 + This 回指 if it were not for... 是固定虚拟句型「要不是因为……」，全句是与现实相反的假设，主句 would be 译「本来会是」；This 回指第 3 段用引用指数评判论文的做法 the famed citation index，译「这种（用引用指数评判的）做法」更清楚。"},{"en":"scientists can easily arrange to cite themselves in their future publications,","lit":"科学家可以轻而易举地安排在自己未来的论文里引用自己","role":"（the fact 的同位语从句（前半））","note":"难点： cite themselves 自引 + arrange to 取义 cite themselves「引用自己（的论文）」即学界所谓「自引」；arrange to 此处是「设法安排去做」；这块点破第 3 段引用指数 the number of times a paper has been quoted 可被人为操纵——自己引自己来刷被引次数。"},{"en":"or get associates to do so for them in return for similar favours.","lit":"或者让同行替他们这样做，以换取类似的回报","role":"（同位语从句（后半，与前半并列））","note":"难点： get sb to do / do so 回指 / in return for / favours get associates to do so 是「让同行（伙伴）这样做」，do so 回指上一块的 cite（即替他们引用）；associates「同行、同事」；in return for similar favours「作为回报、投桃报李」，favours 是「人情、帮忙」，不是「偏爱」。"}]},{"n":50,"en":"If we are serious about ensuring that our science is both meaningful and reproducible, we must ensure that our institutions encourage that kind of science.","refZh":"如果我们真心想确保自己的科学既有意义又能被重复验证，我们就必须确保我们的（科研）制度鼓励的正是这种科学。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"If we are serious about ensuring that our science is both meaningful and reproducible,","lit":"如果我们真的想确保我们的科学既有意义又可重复","role":"（if 条件状语从句（含 about ensuring that 宾语从句））","note":"难点： be serious about 取义 + reproducible 术语 be serious about doing 是「认真对待、真想做某事」，不是「严肃」；reproducible 是科研术语「可重复（验证）的」，指实验结果能被他人重做出来，不可译成「可复制」；both...and... 两项 meaningful、reproducible 都要译出。"},{"en":"we must ensure that our institutions encourage that kind of science.","lit":"我们就必须确保我们的体制鼓励那种科学","role":"（主句 + that 宾语从句）","note":"难点： institutions 取义 + that kind of science 回指 institutions 此处是「（科研）体制、制度／机构」而非「学院」，结合全文批判评价制度，取「制度／体制」更贴；that kind of science 回指本句前半 science is both meaningful and reproducible，须译实成「这种既有意义又可重复的科学」，不能含糊译「那种科学」。"}]}]},{"id":"section3-part-a","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part A 写作","directions":"51. Directions: Suppose you are working for the “Aiding Rural Primary School” project of your university. Write an email to answer the inquiry from an international student volunteer, specifying the details of the project. You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in the email. Use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","prompt":"51. Directions: Suppose you are working for the “Aiding Rural Primary School” project of your university. Write an email to answer the inquiry from an international student volunteer, specifying the details of the project. You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in the email. Use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","points":10,"image":null,"model":{"essay":"Dear Peter, I am Li Ming, a coordinator of the “Aiding Rural Primary School” project. Thank you for your interest in joining us as a volunteer. Here are the details you asked about. This summer, from July 15th to August 15th, our team will travel to a village school in Yunnan Province. Volunteers are expected to teach English, art and basic computer skills to local children. Accommodation and meals will be provided free of charge. If you wish to sign up, please send your resume to aiding@163.com before June 30th. Looking forward to working with you. Yours sincerely, Li Ming","translation":"亲爱的彼得： 我是李明，「助力乡村小学」项目的一名协调员。感谢你有意以志愿者身份加入我们。\n以下是你询问的详情。今年暑假，从七月十五日到八月十五日，我们团队将前往云南省的一所乡村小学。志愿者需要为当地孩子教授英语、美术和基础电脑技能。食宿将免费提供。如果你希望报名，请在六月三十日前把简历发送至 aiding@163.com。\n期待与你共事。 此致 李明","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"身份先行、接住咨询","text":"【第 1 段】 身份先行、接住咨询 。首段用「先认人再接话」：I am Li Ming, a coordinator... 一句交代身份，Thank you for your interest in joining us as a volunteer 正面回应对方的 inquiry，两句就完成了「表明身份+answer the inquiry」，为下文细节开路。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"疑问链坐实全部细节","text":"【第 2 段】 疑问链坐实全部细节 。主体段按「按疑问链铺细节」逐问作答：teach English, art and basic computer skills 答做什么，from July 15th to August 15th... a village school in Yunnan Province 答何时何地，send your resume to aiding@163.com before June 30th 答怎么报名——把 the details of the project 全部说透，句句有实料。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"礼貌收束合规落款","text":"【第 3 段】 礼貌收束合规落款 。结尾用「礼貌收束再落款」：Looking forward to working with you 表达期待，Yours sincerely 敬语后署名 Li Ming，严格遵守 Use “Li Ming” instead，全篇不见真名。"}],"wordCount":100}},{"id":"section3-part-b","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part B 写作","directions":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160-200 words based on the picture below. In your essay, you should 1)describe the picture briefly, 2)interpret the implied meaning, and 3)give your comments. You should write neatly on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points)","prompt":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160-200 words based on the picture below. In your essay, you should 1)describe the picture briefly, 2)interpret the implied meaning, and 3)give your comments. You should write neatly on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points)","points":20,"image":{"src":"assets/writing/2019-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg","alt":"这是一幅标题为“途中”的黑白漫画","width":"616","height":"621","local":"assets/writing/2019-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg"},"model":{"essay":"As is vividly depicted in the cartoon, two hikers are resting on a mountain path. One, still carrying his backpack and leaning on a walking stick, kindly hands a bottle of water to his companion, saying \"Don't give up. Take a rest and then keep climbing.\" The exhausted man, however, sits on the ground with his hands on his chin, complaining \"I'm tired; I won't climb any more.\" Simple as it is, the cartoon conveys a profound message about perseverance. The mountain symbolizes the goals we pursue in life, while the tired man represents those who readily give up when confronted with difficulties. What the drawing truly advocates is that we should not abandon our journey halfway; instead, a short rest, together with encouragement from others, can help us recover and press on toward the summit. In my view, persistence is the key that distinguishes achievers from quitters. History offers countless examples: Thomas Edison failed thousands of times before inventing the light bulb, yet he never stopped. Therefore, whenever we feel like giving up, we should catch our breath, gather our courage and continue climbing, for success always belongs to those who refuse to quit.","translation":"正如漫画生动描绘的那样，两名登山者正在山路上歇脚。其中一人仍背着双肩包、拄着拐杖，善意地把一瓶水递给同伴，说道：“别放弃，休息一下再接着爬。”而那名精疲力竭的人却瘫坐在地、双手托腮，抱怨道：“我累了，不爬了。”\n画面虽简单，却传递出一个关于坚持的深刻寓意。那座山象征着我们人生中追求的目标，而疲惫言弃的人则代表那些一遇困难就轻易放弃的人。漫画真正倡导的是：我们不应半途而废；相反，短暂的休整加上他人的鼓励，能帮我们恢复力气、继续向顶峰进发。\n在我看来，坚持正是区分成功者与放弃者的关键。历史上不乏这样的例子：托马斯·爱迪生在发明电灯前失败了千百次，却从未停下。因此，每当我们想要放弃时，都应先喘口气、鼓起勇气继续攀登，因为成功永远属于那些不肯放弃的人。","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"描图段：一句总起加 while 对比","text":"【第 1 段】 描图段：一句总起加 while 对比 。本段用 As is vividly depicted in the cartoon 开场总起画面，再以 One ..., while ... 的对照把“递水相劝”和“瘫坐言弃”摆在一起，只客观陈述动作与对话，未提前抒情，落实了描述段功能。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"寓意段：象征映射点破深意","text":"【第 2 段】 寓意段：象征映射点破深意 。本段以 Simple as it is 过渡，用 The mountain symbolizes ... while the tired man represents ... 把山与言弃者一一映射到现实，再用 What the drawing truly advocates is that ... 点破倡导坚持的深层寓意，是全篇得分核心。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"评论段：表态加事例加呼吁","text":"【第 3 段】 评论段：表态加事例加呼吁 。本段以 In my view 亮明立场，用 History offers countless examples 引出爱迪生事例作证，末句 Therefore, whenever we feel like giving up, we should ... 收束并回扣中心，做到首尾呼应。"}],"wordCount":195}}],"questionCount":45,"objectiveCount":45,"answerTable":{"ans-section1":"完形填空（四选一） Section I （1–20 题） 1 C Few · 2 C run · 3 B If · 4 D literally · 5 A back · 6 B off · 7 D unfamiliar · 8 C way · 9 A So · 10 D eventually · 11 A surprised · 12 B option · 13 D For example · 14 C spot · 15 B through · 16 D breaks · 17 A artificial · 18 A Finally · 19 B marks · 20 C lead \n（逐题解析见 完形填空（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-1":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (1) （21–25 题） 21 A · 22 D · 23 B · 24 C · 25 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-2":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (2) （26–30 题） 26 D · 27 A · 28 A · 29 C · 30 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-3":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (3) （31–35 题） 31 C · 32 D · 33 B · 34 A · 35 C \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-4":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (4) （36–40 题） 36 C · 37 D · 38 C · 39 B · 40 A \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-b":"段落排序 Section II Part B 41=E → 42=D → 预给F → 43=G → 44=B → 预给C → 45=A \n（逐题解析见 段落排序解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-c":"英译汉（划线句子翻译） Section II Part C \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 英译汉（划线句子翻译）解析页","ans-section3-part-a":"写作 Section III Part A \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页","ans-section3-part-b":"写作 Section III Part B \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页"},"coverage":{"objective":45,"explained":40,"missingSections":[]}};
